* use_again_regression.dts
* USE ... AGAIN: a second work area on an already-open DBF (workspace design
* I5, v1 arm; owner "add use again", 2026-08-12).
*
* Self-bootstrapping and self-erasing: builds its own throwaway x64 tables in
* SANDBOX (UAREGR plain, UAMEMO memo-carrying, UASENT an alias sentinel,
* UAPLAIN for the no-AGAIN alias arm, UAEMP for the self-join), never
* touches live fixtures.
* Every marker is a FIELD-VALUE comparison (FIELDMGR_APPEND doctrine: a shape
* assertion passes green on a blanked table).
*
* What v1 promises, and what each marker holds:
*   UA_G0 -> fixture built (row 1 NAME = Alpha, read back by value)
*   UA_T1a-> duplicate USE WITHOUT AGAIN stays a no-op where a regression
*            would actually land: the TARGET area keeps its sentinel table
*   UA_T1 -> and the already-open area 1 is undisturbed too, by value
*   UA_T2 -> USE ... AGAIN opens a second area on the same file; the second
*            instance reads row 1 by value
*   UA_T3 -> THE COHERENCE MEASUREMENT: a write through area 1 is visible to
*            a re-read through area 2. This marker MEASURES v1 coherence
*            rather than assuming it; if it reds on some platform, that is a
*            finding about two fstreams on one file, not a broken spec.
*   UA_T4 -> AGAIN is REFUSED on a memo-carrying table (two sidecar appenders
*            would interleave -- the AIF-110 shape), and the refusal left the
*            TARGET area untouched: a sentinel table placed there first is
*            still readable by value afterwards.
*   UA_T5 -> the same refusal left the FIRST instance intact, by value.
*   UA_T6 -> USE ... AGAIN ALIAS <n> makes the second instance ADDRESSABLE:
*            SELECT <n> reaches it and reads its row by value.
*   UA_T7 -> a duplicate explicit alias is REFUSED and the target area keeps
*            the different table it already held.
*   UA_T8 -> that refusal did not evict the alias's real holder either.
*   UA_T9 -> ALIAS works WITHOUT AGAIN: a plain open under a chosen name.
*   UA_T10-> an all-digit alias is refused, target area untouched.
*   UA_T11-> ALIAS with no name following is refused, target area untouched.
*   UA_T12 -> THE SELF-JOIN, and the arms that test what the verb is FOR:
*             two aliased cursors on ONE table, related to each other.
*             T12 declaring the relation positions the child;
*   UA_T12b-> and the child TRACKS the parent when the parent moves. Two
*             markers because they are two guarantees: a one-shot applied at
*             declaration would leave T12 green and T12b red.
*
* Read the T4 site before editing it. It took three cuts, the first two of
* which could not fail for their stated reason, and the third of which
* required fixing the guard it tests.
*
* THE RECURRING DEFECT IN THIS FILE -- T4 twice, T6, T1, and a draft of T8,
* all on the day it was written, and no count is given because that number
* moved twice while this paragraph was being edited. One shape throughout:
* ASSERTING A POSTCONDITION FROM A POSITION WHERE IT ALREADY HELD. Each was
* written directly after the operation it checks, so the cursor, the area, or
* the value it wanted was already in place before that operation ran -- which
* means the operation could have done nothing at all and the marker would not
* have noticed. The check that catches it is mechanical and
* cheap: BEFORE trusting a green, ask what state the marker would report if
* the command under test were deleted outright. If the answer is "the same
* green", the marker is decoration. Two of these were caught only by reading
* the transcript of a passing run, which is why the transcript gets read.
*
* WHY ALIAS IS HERE AT ALL (owner, 2026-08-12): without it USE AGAIN produced
* a second cursor that was open and UNREACHABLE BY NAME, because both
* instances took the file stem and find_open_area_by_name_ci first-matches
* silently. Naming is how SET RELATION declares a join, so an unnameable
* cursor cannot do the one job a second cursor exists for.
*
* RECCOUNT (owner correction, same day, and a correction to the correction):
* the count IS a stored field in the DBF header (bytes 4-7), reachable as
* DbArea::recCount64(), and it is now surfaced beside DELETED in
* glue_xbase.cpp -- but that serves compile_predicate, i.e. scan and FOR
* clauses, NOT the '?' marker path, which is rhs_eval. So RECCOUNT is real
* and usable in a FOR predicate over an OPEN area, and unavailable to these
* markers. Stated because the first version of this note claimed otherwise.
*
* v1 boundaries, deliberate and stated: the AGAIN instance opens in PHYSICAL
* ORDER (a second in-process attach of one container would double-open an
* LMDB environment -- undefined by LMDB's contract), and memo tables refuse.
* Index-attach-on-AGAIN and memo-share-on-AGAIN are later, separately-gated
* arms; when either lands, this spec gains an arm rather than losing one.
*
* Run:  REGRESSION RUN USE_AGAIN
*   or: DOTSCRIPT dottalkpp/data/scripts/use_again_regression.dts

SET ECHO OFF
STOP_ON_ERROR OFF

* Isolate to SANDBOX (house convention: every test sets its own path slots).
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX
SET PATH INDEXES INDEXES/SANDBOX
SET PATH LMDB LMDB/SANDBOX

FORMULA "USE-AGAIN-REGRESSION-BEGIN"

* --- P0: throwaway fixture (idempotent: erase any prior leftovers first) ---
WORKSPACE CLOSE
ERASE TABLE UAREGR CONFIRM
ERASE TABLE UAMEMO CONFIRM
ERASE TABLE UASENT CONFIRM
ERASE TABLE UAPLAIN CONFIRM
ERASE TABLE UAEMP CONFIRM

SELECT 1
CREATE X64 UAREGR (ID N(4), NAME C(20))
APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH "1"
REPLACE NAME WITH "Alpha"
GO 1
? "UA_G0_fixture_row:" + (NAME = "Alpha")

* Sentinel table, built once here and used by T1 and T7. It carries a value
* NO other fixture has ("Sentinel"), which is the whole point: a sentinel that
* shares a value with the table under test cannot discriminate.
SELECT 6
CREATE X64 UASENT (ID N(4), NAME C(20))
APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH "6"
REPLACE NAME WITH "Sentinel"

* --- T1: duplicate USE without AGAIN is a no-op; BOTH areas undisturbed ----
* T1a is the marker that actually tests the refusal. Its first cut asserted
* only that area 1 survived -- true even if the guard were deleted and area 2
* had opened a second instance, since that would not touch area 1. So area 2
* is given a sentinel first: the refusal must leave UASENT sitting there. If
* the guard goes, area 2 becomes UAREGR, NAME reads Alpha, and T1a reds.
SELECT 2
USE UASENT AGAIN ALIAS T1GUARD
USE UAREGR
GO 1
? "UA_T1a_dup_refused_target_area_untouched:" + (NAME = "Sentinel")

SELECT 1
GO 1
? "UA_T1_dup_refused_area1_intact:" + (NAME = "Alpha")

* --- T2: AGAIN opens a second area on the same file ------------------------
SELECT 2
USE UAREGR AGAIN
GO 1
? "UA_T2_second_area_reads:" + (NAME = "Alpha")

* --- T3: coherence MEASUREMENT -- write via area 1, re-read via area 2 -----
SELECT 1
GO 1
REPLACE NAME WITH "Alpha2"
SELECT 2
GO 1
? "UA_T3_write_visible_across_areas:" + (NAME = "Alpha2")

* --- T4: memo-carrying table refuses AGAIN; first instance intact ----------
SELECT 3
CREATE X64 UAMEMO (ID N(4), NOTES M)
APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH "7"
* T4 asserts the refusal by SENTINEL OCCUPANCY, and the technique is the
* point. Three cuts were needed to get here (2026-08-12):
*
*   cut 1  .NOT. (ID = 7) in the empty target area -- a green that could not
*          fail: ID was UNRESOLVABLE there, so the marker passed whether or
*          not the refusal fired.
*   cut 2  RECCOUNT() = 0 in the same empty area -- errored, because the
*          marker evaluator binds a NULL AREA unless the area is OPEN
*          (rhs_eval.cpp:969). Being closed is exactly what was being
*          asserted, so NO symbol can resolve there: not RECCOUNT, not
*          RECNO, not a field name.
*   cut 3  this one. Stop asking whether the area is EMPTY -- an
*          unaskable question -- and ask whether a KNOWN OCCUPANT SURVIVED,
*          which is asked of an OPEN area and therefore answerable.
*
* Two general findings, both outliving this spec. IN THIS MARKER LANGUAGE NO
* MARKER CAN ASSERT THAT AN AREA IS EMPTY; every such attempt is a false
* green or a silent error. And AN ERRORED MARKER PRINTS NOTHING -- it does
* not go red, it goes ABSENT, so a suite scored by counting greens still
* reads full while a claim has quietly left it.
*
* Cut 3 also required a SOURCE FIX, and found it: the refusal used to run
* AFTER reset_area_runtime_best_effort() and a.open(), so it destroyed this
* area's occupant and then printed "Nothing was opened". The sentinel could
* not have survived a guard that wiped the area before deciding to refuse.
* Hoisted into the duplicate-open guard (cmd_use.cpp), where AGAIN's own
* precondition -- the file is already open elsewhere -- makes the memo probe
* free: it reads the live area's field list and touches no filesystem.

SELECT 4
USE UAREGR AGAIN

* Area 4 now holds a KNOWN occupant: UAREGR, whose row 1 reads Alpha2 (T3
* wrote it). This is the sentinel.
USE UAMEMO AGAIN

* The refusal must leave the sentinel standing. If the memo guard regresses
* -- removed, or sunk back below the open -- area 4 is reset and reopened as
* UAMEMO, NAME ceases to exist there, and this reds. That is a marker that
* can only be green for the reason it claims.
GO 1
? "UA_T4_memo_refusal_left_target_area_untouched:" + (NAME = "Alpha2")

SELECT 3
GO 1
? "UA_T5_memo_refusal_left_first_intact:" + (ID = 7)

* --- T6/T7/T8: ALIAS (owner 'fix the use command', 2026-08-12) -------------
* Before this arm, USE AGAIN produced a second instance that WAS OPEN AND
* UNREACHABLE BY NAME: both instances took the file stem as their logical
* name, and find_open_area_by_name_ci (workarea_util.cpp:29) returns the
* FIRST match with no diagnostic -- so SET RELATION and every name-based verb
* silently bound the lower-numbered area. A cursor you cannot name is not
* usable for relations, which is the whole point of opening it.

SELECT 5
USE UAREGR AGAIN ALIAS SECONDLOOK
GO 1

* T6: the explicit alias took, and the instance answers to it. Asserted by
* VALUE through the alias, not by reading the alias back -- an area reporting
* its own name proves the string was stored, not that anything resolves it.
*
* THE SELECT 3 IS LOAD-BEARING. The first cut of this marker ran
* SELECT SECONDLOOK while the cursor was ALREADY in area 5, so a total
* failure of alias resolution would have left it exactly where the marker
* wanted it and the assertion would have gone green regardless. Caught by
* reading its own transcript after it passed. Stepping to area 3 first means
* the cursor MUST move for this to read Alpha2: UAMEMO has no NAME field, so
* a SELECT that does not resolve leaves the marker on an empty string.
SELECT 3
SELECT SECONDLOOK
GO 1
? "UA_T6_alias_is_addressable_by_name:" + (NAME = "Alpha2")

* T7: a duplicate explicit alias is REFUSED, and the refusal leaves the
* target area untouched. The sentinel here is a DIFFERENT TABLE on purpose:
* the first draft of this marker put UAREGR in area 6, so its value matched
* area 5's and the marker would have gone green even if SELECT had failed and
* left the cursor elsewhere. Distinct values are what make it discriminate.
SELECT 6
USE UAREGR AGAIN ALIAS SECONDLOOK
GO 1
? "UA_T7_duplicate_alias_refused_area_untouched:" + (NAME = "Sentinel")

* T8: the refusal did not evict the alias's real HOLDER either -- area 5 still
* answers to SECONDLOOK. If the name had been taken away, SELECT would fail,
* the cursor would stay in area 6, and this reds on "Sentinel".
SELECT SECONDLOOK
GO 1
? "UA_T8_refusal_left_alias_holder_intact:" + (NAME = "Alpha2")

* --- T9/T10/T11: the ALIAS paths the first pass left untested --------------
* Written because three branches of the new clause had no marker at all:
* ALIAS without AGAIN, the all-digit refusal, and the malformed clause. Every
* arm above exercised AGAIN ALIAS together, so "the alias code works" rested
* on one of its four paths. An untested refusal is the worst kind: it is
* supposed to do nothing visible, so it looks fine while doing nothing at all.

* T9: ALIAS with NO AGAIN. Needs a table not already open, so UAPLAIN is
* built and then closed before being re-opened under a chosen name.
SELECT 8
CREATE X64 UAPLAIN (ID N(4), NAME C(20))
APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH "9"
REPLACE NAME WITH "Plain"
WORKSPACE CLOSE 8
* WORKSPACE CLOSE <n>, not SCHEMAS CLOSE <n>: the latter works but prints a
* deprecation banner. Found by this spec running it -- the USE duplicate-open
* message had been RECOMMENDING the deprecated verb, and was corrected with
* this arm (helpdata_messages.cpp).

SELECT 8
USE UAPLAIN ALIAS PLAINNAME
SELECT 3
SELECT PLAINNAME
GO 1
? "UA_T9_alias_without_again_resolves:" + (NAME = "Plain")

* T10: an all-digit alias is refused -- SELECT would read it as an area
* number, so the name would look bound and silently select a slot. Area 7
* holds a sentinel; the refusal must leave it there.
SELECT 7
USE UASENT AGAIN ALIAS GUARD7
USE UAREGR AGAIN ALIAS 42
GO 1
? "UA_T10_all_digit_alias_refused_area_untouched:" + (NAME = "Sentinel")

* T11: ALIAS with nothing after it is refused rather than silently read as
* "no alias given" -- those two readings differ by which name the table ends
* up under, which is exactly the kind of difference that goes unnoticed.
USE UAREGR AGAIN ALIAS
GO 1
? "UA_T11_malformed_alias_refused_area_untouched:" + (NAME = "Sentinel")

* --- T12: THE SELF-JOIN. What USE AGAIN is FOR. ---------------------------
* Every arm above tests the plumbing: that a second instance opens, is named,
* and refuses what it should. None tests the PURPOSE. A second cursor on one
* table exists so a row can be read against ANOTHER ROW OF THE SAME TABLE --
* the self-join -- and until that is measured, this lane has shipped a
* mechanism with no demonstrated use.
*
* This is possible only because of two facts measured 2026-08-12, either of
* which could have blocked it:
*   - SET RELATION in this engine matches BY FIELD VALUE THROUGH A SCAN
*     (set_relations.cpp:304-322), not by an index seek. v1's AGAIN forces
*     physical order, so an index-seeking relation would have been unusable
*     on exactly the instances this feature creates.
*   - relations resolve the child through find_open_area_by_name_ci, which
*     compares the logical name. Before ALIAS both instances carried the file
*     stem, so INTO <name> could not have addressed the second one at all.
*
* THIS ARM IS A MEASUREMENT, in the sense T3 is. It asserts that positioning
* the PARENT moves the CHILD to its related row. If it reds, the finding is
* that this engine's relations do not follow the cursor for a single-file
* self-join -- worth knowing exactly, and not the same thing as a broken
* spec. Do not "fix" a red here by weakening the marker.

* TWO CORRECTIONS, both from the first run of this arm (2026-08-12):
*
*  1. CREATE ALREADY OPENS the table in the current area, so the USE that
*     followed it was refused as "already open in current area" and its ALIAS
*     was silently dropped. The alias never existed, SELECT on it failed, the
*     cursor stayed where it was, and SET RELATION then ran FROM THE CHILD
*     INTO ITSELF. The area keeps the name CREATE gave it, so the parent is
*     addressed as UAEMP and no second name is needed.
*  2. THIS ENGINE JOINS ON A COMMON FIELD NAME -- parent.F to child.F, as
*     "REL: BOSS -> BOSS ON MGR" reported and the MCC precedent confirms
*     (SET RELATION TO SID INTO ENROLL, SID on both sides). A MGR -> ID
*     hierarchy is therefore INEXPRESSIBLE, so the original Ada/Bob design
*     could not have worked even with the alias bug fixed. The join here is
*     on DEPT: rows that SHARE a department, which is what an equi-join on
*     one field name means for a single table.
*
* Also found and NOT fixed here, because it is the relation engine's business
* rather than USE's: a relation from an area INTO ITSELF was accepted and
* reported OK. A one-node cycle is not a join.

SELECT 9
CREATE X64 UAEMP (ID N(4), NAME C(20), DEPT N(4))
APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH "1"
REPLACE NAME WITH "Ada"
REPLACE DEPT WITH "10"
APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH "2"
REPLACE NAME WITH "Bob"
REPLACE DEPT WITH "20"
APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH "3"
REPLACE NAME WITH "Cid"
REPLACE DEPT WITH "10"

* Second cursor on the same file, under its own name.
SELECT 10
USE UAEMP AGAIN ALIAS BOSS

* Park the child on Bob (record 2, DEPT 20). If the relation does NOT follow
* the parent, the child stays here and the marker reads Bob -- which is the
* whole reason it is parked somewhere wrong first.
GO 2

SELECT 9
SET RELATION TO DEPT INTO BOSS
GO 3

* Parent is Cid, DEPT 10. The child must move to the first row sharing that
* department: Ada, record 1. Three rows exist so the match is unambiguous and
* is neither the parent's own record nor the child's parked one.
SELECT BOSS
? "UA_T12_selfjoin_relation_positions_child:" + (NAME = "Ada")

* T12b: THE PARENT ACTUALLY MOVES. T12 alone does not prove this, and its
* first name claimed it did: the third APPEND had already left the parent on
* record 3, so the GO 3 above was a no-op and all T12 measured was that
* DECLARING the relation positions the child once. Whether the child TRACKS
* the parent is a separate guarantee, and it is the one a self-join needs.
*
* Parent moves 3 -> 2 (Cid, DEPT 10 -> Bob, DEPT 20). The only row with DEPT
* 20 is Bob himself, record 2, so the child must leave record 1 and land
* there. If the relation is a one-shot applied at declaration, the child
* stays on Ada and this reds while T12 stays green -- which is exactly the
* distinction worth having two markers for.
SELECT 9
GO 2
SELECT BOSS
? "UA_T12b_selfjoin_child_tracks_parent_movement:" + (NAME = "Bob")

SET RELATION OFF ALL

FORMULA "USE-AGAIN-REGRESSION-END"

* --- teardown: close everything, erase the throwaways ----------------------
WORKSPACE CLOSE
ERASE TABLE UAREGR CONFIRM
ERASE TABLE UAMEMO CONFIRM
ERASE TABLE UASENT CONFIRM
ERASE TABLE UAPLAIN CONFIRM
ERASE TABLE UAEMP CONFIRM
